digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56413-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,3,0) Source: (1, 3, 0) Transcription: Any source generating current I=ef provides a realization of the ampere."];
  "N10" [label="(6,2,0) Source: (6, 2, 0) Transcription: Sharp current steps could be promising candidates as quantum sources, although their flatness is still in debate."];
  "N12" [label="(8,2,0) Source: (8, 2, 0) Transcription: The Josephson effect provides a direct and universal realization of the volt from h/2e."];
  "N13" [label="(8,3,0) Source: (8, 3, 0) Transcription: The quantum Hall effect provides a direct and universal realization of the ohm from h/e^2."];
  "N14" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: JV realizes volt and QHR realizes ohm, so JV and QHR provide quantized V and R."];
  "N15" [label="(9,2,0) Source: (9, 2, 0) Transcription: The high accuracy of Josephson voltage standards relies on the phase rigidity of macroscopic superconductors."];
  "N16" [label="(11,2,0) Source: (11, 2, 0) Transcription: The accurate application of quantized voltage uses the charge rigidity of the quantum Hall edge states."];
  "N19" [label="(13,1,0) Source: (13, 1, 0) Transcription: A calculable current of 1 μA has been generated from the series connection of both quantum standards."];
  "N2" [label="(1,2,1) Source: (1, 2, 1) Transcription: The revised SI fixes the elementary charge e as a fundamental constant."];
  "N20" [label="(13,3,0) Source: (13, 3, 0) Transcription: The accuracy of the 1 μA current measurement was due to the very low resistance of the ammeter."];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Current generated but accuracy depended on ammeter, so series connection measurement has limitations."];
  "N22" [label="(15,2,0) Source: (15, 2, 0) Transcription: The PQCG locks an electron flow to the current circulating in the loop formed by the quantum standards."];
  "N23" [label="(16,1,0) Source: (16, 1, 0) Transcription: The accuracy of the PQCG was demonstrated in the milliampere range."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: PQCG locks flow and demonstrated accuracy, so PQCG has capability for quantum current generation."];
  "N25" [label="(17,2,0) Source: (17, 2, 0) Transcription: The corrections were due to the non-ideal connection of the quantum standards."];
  "N26" [label="(18,1,0) Source: (18, 1, 0) Transcription: The final uncertainty of the new quantum current standard was impaired by corrections."];
  "N29" [label="(14,1,0) Source: (14, 1, 0) Transcription: The main issue is to implement the accurate series connection of the two quantum standards."];
  "N31" [label="(20,1,0) Source: (20, 1, 0) Transcription: The performance results from the implementation of a three-terminal connection of the QHRS."];
  "N32" [label="(20,2,0) Source: (20, 2, 0) Transcription: The three-terminal connection allows a highly-accurate application of the Josephson voltage to the quantized resistance."];
  "N34" [label="(19,3,0) Source: (19, 3, 0) Transcription: The next-generation PQCG delivers currents without any classical correction."];
  "N36" [label="(21,1,0) Source: (21, 1, 0) Transcription: The realization of the ampere is demonstrated with relative uncertainties below 10^-8."];
  "N38" [label="(22,1,0) Source: (22, 1, 0) Transcription: The realization is achieved using a full quantum instrumentation made of five quantum devices."];
  "N4" [label="(2,3,0) Source: (2, 3, 0) Transcription: Single-electron current sources (SECS) are often presented as the most obvious way to realize the ampere."];
  "N5" [label="(3,2,0) Source: (3, 2, 0) Transcription: Achieving accurate currents above 100 pA using SECS is challenging due to increasing error rates at high frequencies."];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: SECS is obvious way but has high error rates, so SECS realization is challenging for high accuracy."];
  "N7" [label="(4,2,0) Source: (4, 2, 0) Transcription: Dual Shapiro steps have been evidenced in superconducting nanowires under microwave radiation."];
  "N8" [label="(5,1,0) Source: (5, 1, 0) Transcription: Enhanced phase variance allows photon-assisted tunneling of fluxons and synchronized transfer of Cooper pairs."];
  "N9" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Dual steps exist and allow transfer, so direct quantum current steps are physically possible."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper develops and evaluates a next-generation programmable quantum current generator for primary realization of the SI ampere, using a three-terminal connection of a quantum Hall resistance standard to apply Josephson-array voltages to quantized resistance and deliver quantized currents without classical corrections, with demonstrated relative uncertainties below 10^-8 across the microampere to milliampere range.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A practical primary ampere realization is motivated when prior direct-current approaches have limitations, quantum voltage and resistance standards can generate quantized current, and a connection design enables accurate operation without classical corrections."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) In the revised SI, a source generating a current of the form I = ef can provide a realization of the ampere."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Corrections associated with the non-ideal connection of the quantum standards impaired the final uncertainty of the quantum current standard."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) SECS realization at high accuracy and direct current-step approaches both face specific technical challenges."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The revised-SI route to realizing the ampere through I = ef, together with challenges in some direct current-source approaches, motivates investigation of a robust quantum-current realization."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The Josephson-voltage and quantum-Hall-resistance combination is a promising route for a robust quantized-current realization of the ampere."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) A PQCG architecture that locks electron flow to a loop formed by quantum standards is a plausible platform for using Josephson-voltage and quantum-Hall-resistance standards in quantized-current generation."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) A PQCG approach based on quantum standards is plausible, but connection-related corrections that impair uncertainty motivate an accurate series connection without such corrections."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Because connection-related corrections motivate an accurate series connection and the main issue is implementing the accurate series connection of the two quantum standards, the design goal is to solve the connection issue."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The implemented three-terminal connection of the QHRS enables highly accurate application of the Josephson voltage to the quantized resistance."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The three-terminal QHRS connection addresses the connection issue by enabling highly accurate application of the Josephson voltage to the quantized resistance."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The next-generation PQCG is reported to deliver currents without classical correction, consistent with the connection-focused design solution."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) The next-generation PQCG combines delivery of currents without classical correction with a demonstrated realization of the ampere at relative uncertainties below 10^-8."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) The demonstrated next-generation PQCG performance supports the Josephson-voltage and quantum-Hall-resistance route as a practical approach to robust quantized-current realization."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The reported realization uses full quantum instrumentation made of five quantum devices and supports a next-generation PQCG approach to ampere realization."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Direct current-step approaches are physically plausible candidates for quantum current sources, but the flatness of such sharp current steps remains debated."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Josephson voltage standards and quantum Hall resistance applications each derive high accuracy from rigidity phenomena: phase rigidity in superconductors and charge rigidity in quantum Hall edge states."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because Josephson and quantum Hall standards provide quantized voltage and resistance with accuracy grounded in their respective rigidity phenomena, their combination can support accurate quantized current generation."];
